-- title:
--   Theorem 4.2 — universal quantum inductive inference with round complexity $O(s\epsilon^{-2}\delta^{-1})$
-- statement:
--   There is a constant $C$ such that for every source model there is a single learner $\mathcal L$ (a family of quantum channels that sees only the parameters, the history, the next instrument and one copy of the post-measurement state) with the following property. For all $n,s,t\in\mathbb N$, all $\epsilon=1/a$ and $\delta=1/b$ with $a,b\in\mathbb N_{\ge1}$, all $m\ge1$ with
--
--   $$
--   m\ \ge\ C\cdot\frac{s}{\epsilon^{2}\delta},
--   $$
--
--   every source $\rho$ generated by a program of length at most $s$, and every sequence of instruments $\mathcal I_1,\dots,\mathcal I_m$ (arbitrary finite outcome sets, arbitrary post-measurement maps),
--
--   $$
--   \Pr_{\substack{i\leftarrow[m]\\ x_{<i}\leftarrow D^{\mathcal I,\rho}_{<i}}}\Big[\mathrm{TD}\big(\mathcal L(\mathsf{param},h_{<i},\mathcal I_i,\rho_{<i|h<i}),\ \tau_{\le i|h<i}\big)\le\epsilon\Big]\ \ge\ 1-\delta .
--   $$
--
--   The number of rounds needed does not depend on the register size $n$, the time bound $t$ or the instruments. The learner predicts the joint state of the past registers, the next register and the next outcome, not only their marginal. The bound matches the classical Solomonoff rate $O(s/(\epsilon^2\delta))$, and Theorem A.1 of the paper shows it is optimal.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The learner may depend on the source model (the paper's learner knows the universal machine and gate set), but $C$ may not. The source class is $\{\rho^{T}_{R_1\dots R_m}:|T|\le s\}$ for the given model.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: sources in C_{n,m}[s,t]: states prepared by a circuit that a Turing machine T with |T| ≤ s outputs within t steps, over a fixed gate set and encoding (Eq. (50), pp.15–16) → every source model gen : (n t : ℕ) → List Bool → QuantumProcess n, sources (gen n t T).state m with |T| ≤ s; C quantified before gen; reason: the paper's encoding (with the padding/tracing convention of Eq. (40)) is one source model, so instantiating gen gives the paper's class; C uniform over models is stronger (paper.md R1)
--   - reading: a quantum algorithm L → a Learner: a family of quantum channels (finite Kraus form), one per classical input; reason: Theorem 4.2 is information theoretic (§4.2 title), its learner is an integral of rotated Petz maps and no efficiency is claimed; a channel is the information-theoretic notion of a quantum algorithm acting on one copy (paper.md R3)
--   - reading: round complexity r(s, ε⁻¹, δ⁻¹) = O(sε⁻²δ⁻¹) → r = C·s·a²·b for one constant C, for all s, a, b (including s = 0); reason: the stronger reading of O(·) (bound for all arguments, not only large ones); it is what the proof gives (Eq. (76))
--   - generalization: instruments specified by circuit descriptions (over the fixed gate set), finite outcome sets X_j → every instrument given by finite Kraus families, outcomes Fin k_j; reason: every finite set is in bijection with some Fin k and every implementable instrument has a finite Kraus family, so the paper's instruments are among those quantified over
--   - encoding: the learner receives the circuit descriptions of the instruments → the learner receives the instruments themselves (Kraus families); reason: a description determines its instrument, so a learner that uses only the instrument is a learner in the paper's sense
--   - encoding: ε⁻¹, δ⁻¹ ∈ ℕ → a, b ∈ ℕ with 1 ≤ a, 1 ≤ b, ε = 1/a, δ = 1/b; reason: same parameter range; a, b ≥ 1 only excludes ε⁻¹ = 0 or δ⁻¹ = 0, which are undefined
--   - encoding: i ← [m] uniformly → hypothesis 1 ≤ m; reason: the uniform choice over [m] is undefined for m = 0 (possible when s = 0)
--   - encoding: TD(ρ, σ) = ½‖ρ − σ‖₁ → (1/2) * WildeQIT.traceDist; reason: the published traceDist is the unnormalized ‖·‖₁
--   - encoding: registers R_i of n qubits; outcome register written first in M_I → Fin (2^n) per register, register R_i = coordinate i−1; output indexed (past registers) × (next register × outcome); reason: relabelling of the index set; changes no trace, trace distance or relative entropy
--   - encoding: conditional states defined for histories of positive probability → 0⁻¹ = 0 makes them the zero matrix on histories of probability zero; reason: those histories have weight zero in every sum; only undefined cases are affected
-- source:
--   Hiroka, Hsieh, Shirakawa, arXiv:2609.36912 (PDF of 2026-11-02), Theorem 4.2, p.19 (= Theorem 1.1, p.5), with Definition 4.1, Eq. (56), p.19, and Eq. (50), p.18

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QIndInf_successProb

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace QIndInf

open QAlgorithms.CopyMeasurements

/-- **Theorem 4.2 (universal quantum inductive inference, round complexity `O(s ε⁻² δ⁻¹)`).**
There is a constant `C` such that for every source model there is a single learner `L` (a family
of quantum channels that sees only the parameters, the measurement history, the next instrument
and one copy of the post-measurement state) with the following property. For all register sizes
`n`, program-length bounds `s`, time bounds `t`, accuracies `ε = 1/a` and confidences `δ = 1/b`
(`a, b ≥ 1`), every number of rounds `m ≥ 1` with `m ≥ C · s · ε⁻² · δ⁻¹`, every source generated by
a program of length at most `s`, and every sequence of instruments `I₁, …, I_m` with arbitrary
finite outcome sets,
`Pr_{i ← [m], x<i ← D^{I,ρ}_{<i}} [TD(L(param, h<i, I_i, ρ_{<i|h<i}), τ_{≤i|h<i}) ≤ ε] ≥ 1 − δ`.

arXiv:2609.36912 (PDF of 2026-11-02), Theorem 4.2, p.19 (= Theorem 1.1, p.5), with Definition 4.1,
p.19, unfolded; the source class is Eq. (50), p.18.

**Formalization Note.**
* The paper's class `C_{n,m}[s,t]` (Turing machines `T` with `|T| ≤ s` whose `t`-step run outputs a
  circuit preparing the state, p.15–16 conventions) is `{(gen n t T).state m : |T| ≤ s}` for the one
  source model fixed by the paper's gate set and encoding. The statement holds for every source
  model; the learner may depend on the model (the paper's learner knows `U` and `G`), but `C` may
  not.
* `O(s ε⁻² δ⁻¹)` is the round complexity `r(s, a, b) = C · s · a² · b`, with one constant `C`
  uniform in `n`, `t`, the source model, the source and the instruments.
* `m ≥ 1` is required because `i` is drawn uniformly from `[m]`.
* The learner (`Learner`) does not receive `m`, the program, the source or later instruments.
* `TD = ½ · WildeQIT.traceDist`; see `successProb`. -/
theorem universal_quantum_inductive_inference :
    ∃ C : ℕ, ∀ gen : SourceModel, ∃ L : Learner,
      ∀ (n s t a b m : ℕ), 1 ≤ a → 1 ≤ b → 1 ≤ m → C * s * a ^ 2 * b ≤ m →
        ∀ T : List Bool, T.length ≤ s →
          ∀ (k : Fin m → ℕ) (I : (j : Fin m) → QuantumInstrument (Reg n) (Fin (k j))),
            1 - 1 / (b : ℝ) ≤ successProb L s t a b (gen n t T) k I := by sorry

end QIndInf
